WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.678
IaC security improves when teams check
changes before they reach production.

2
00:00:07.758 --> 00:00:14.719
Static analysis, policy-as-code, schema
validation, required metadata,

3
00:00:14.799 --> 00:00:17.953
naming rules, tagging standards,

4
00:00:18.033 --> 00:00:23.797
and environment-specific checks can catch
many issues early.

5
00:00:23.877 --> 00:00:31.382
These checks turn repeated review
questions into consistent automation without

6
00:00:31.462 --> 00:00:36.465
removing human judgment from high-impact
decisions.

7
00:00:36.545 --> 00:00:42.962
Guardrails can look for missing
encryption, broad network exposure,

8
00:00:43.042 --> 00:00:49.023
weak identity scope, missing logging,
public storage settings,

9
00:00:49.103 --> 00:00:53.454
unsupported regions, missing ownership
tags,

10
00:00:53.534 --> 00:00:57.776
or environment-specific policy violations.

11
00:00:57.856 --> 00:01:03.620
They can also enforce secure defaults and
reusable standards.

12
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:10.660
The best guardrails focus on settings that
matter and provide feedback that

13
00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:13.895
engineers can understand and fix.

14
00:01:13.975 --> 00:01:21.044
Exceptions should be visible, documented,
and approved by the right owners.

15
00:01:21.124 --> 00:01:24.604
Some exceptions may be time-bounded.

16
00:01:24.684 --> 00:01:31.101
Others may require compensating controls
or additional monitoring.

17
00:01:31.181 --> 00:01:36.510
An undocumented exception becomes a quiet
policy bypass.

18
00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:43.551
A documented exception becomes a decision
that can be reviewed, revisited,

19
00:01:43.631 --> 00:01:47.873
and retired when the underlying need
changes.

20
00:01:47.953 --> 00:01:51.868
Guardrails should help teams move safely,

21
00:01:51.948 --> 00:01:56.516
not become hidden magic that nobody
understands.

22
00:01:56.596 --> 00:01:58.228
If a policy fails,

23
00:01:58.308 --> 00:02:04.616
the message should explain the issue and
the expected safer pattern.

24
00:02:04.696 --> 00:02:08.937
If a rule is too noisy, teams may work
around it.

25
00:02:09.017 --> 00:02:16.304
Maintainable policy-as-code balances
enforcement with clarity, ownership,

26
00:02:16.384 --> 00:02:23.780
and continuous improvement based on real
failures, drift, and review findings.
